From 534b2ac307b6f52dedda835dd1685748e723ee48 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kuzma30 <kuzemkoa@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 10:09:17 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 09/24] Modeling Algorithms - Close fillet corners at the start
 of a thread on a split cylinder

At the start of a thread cut by a helix (FreeCAD issue 32486) a fillet may
end on a cylinder that is split into two faces sharing one surface, with
the smooth edge between them starting at the end vertex. The common edge
then has both pcurves on that surface in one closed representation.

ChFi3d_CoutureOnVertex took such an edge for a seam because it is closed
on the surface. The vertex was counted with an extra face and the face
walk in PerformIntersectionAtEnd ran past the last edge ("Failed to find
edge"). Only an edge used twice by the face is a seam now. The rebuilt
piece of the edge between the two faces could then get its pcurves
swapped, which left both faces with wires jumping by the period; after the
build such pcurves are swapped back when the other one joins the wire and
the current one does not.

PerformTwoCorner builds the corner of a convex and a concave fillet (one
stripe OnSame, the other OnDiff) from the crossing of their traces on the
common face. There the convex fillet ends with a short surfdata that stops
before the trace of the concave one, and the corner failed with "No
intersection pc pc". The neighbouring surfdata were searched only when the
stripes did not face each other at all; they are searched now also when
they face each other but their traces do not cross. Without a crossing
the end surfdata are restored and the old failure stays.

The corner curve on the face opposite to the common one ends on the arc
where the concave fillet leaves its support. That arc must bound this
face, but the check did not work and here it bounds another face on the
same surface, joined to this one by a smooth edge. In that case the piece
of the arc from the corner to the vertex is added to both faces, as
PerformIntersectionAtEnd does for such pieces, and the spine edge of the
OnSame fillet is cut at the vertex, as PerformOneCorner does for an OnSame
end. Without that the face kept the spine edge and the shell was open.

Tests bugs/modalg_8/fillet_thread_end_on_split_cylinder and
fillet_thread_convex_concave_corner.

